The collector current is given by the diffusion current relation: \[I_c = AqD_n\frac{dn}{dx}, \quad \text{where } D_n = \mu_n V_T\] Substituting \(D_n = \mu_n V_T = 800 \times 26 \times 10^{-3}\,\text{cm}^2/\text{s}\) and the given values , \(dn/dx = 10^{14} / (0.5 \times 10^{-4})\,\text{cm}^{-4}\): \[I_c = 0.001 \times (1.6 \times 10^{-19}) \times 800 \times 26 \times 10^{-3}
              \times \left[\frac{10^{14}}{0.5 \times 10^{-4}}\right] = 66.56 \times 10^{-4}\,\text{A}
              = 6.656 \text{ mA}\] \[\boxed{6.656}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>The collector current in a BJT is dominated by minority carrier diffusion across the base.</li><li>The carrier gradient \(dn/dx\) is approximated as \(\Delta n / W_B\), where \(W_B\) is the effective base width.</li></ul></div>

Given circuit is, <img src="img/18e7ee41c23dacc0.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,320px)"> In the combination of two Diode circuits at a time, only one Diode will turn ON. When \(V_0 = +V_{sat}\), then \(D_1\) is ON and \(D_2\) is OFF. When \(V_0 = -V_{sat}\) then \(D_2\) is ON and \(D_1\) is OFF. So the given circuit introduces amplitude stabilization by preventing the Op-Amp from saturating and thus producing sinusoidal oscillations of fixed amplitude. In other words, the circuit shown is a Wein bridge oscillator. The amplitude of oscillations can be determined and stabilized by using a nonlinear control network. As the oscillations grow, the diodes start to conduct causing the effective resistance in the feedback to decrease. Equilibrium will be reached at the output amplitude that causes the loop gain to be exactly unity. \[\boxed{
\parbox{0.85\linewidth}{
(A)\quad introduce amplitude stabilization by preventing the op amp from saturating and thus producing sinusoidal oscillations of fixed amplitude
}
}\]

We know that: \[|X_C| = \frac{1}{\omega C} = \frac{1}{2\pi f C}\] The parasitic capacitances are in pF (\(10^{-12}\,\text{F}\)). So, \(|X_C|\) becomes too large (\(\approx \infty\)) for the mid freq. band so parasitic capacitance act like open circuit. The bypass and coupling capacitances are in \(\mu\text{F}\) (\(10^{-6}\,\text{F}\)) so, \(X_C\) becomes very small (\(\approx 0\)) for the mid-frequency band so, they behave as a short circuit. Hence, the correct option is (A). \[\boxed{
\begin{array}{l}
\text{(A)}\quad \text{The device parasitic capacitances behave like open circuits,}\\
\text{whereas coupling and bypass capacitances behave like short circuits.}
\end{array}
}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li><strong>Mid-Band Operation:</strong> In the mid-band frequency range of an amplifier, the frequency is sufficiently high such that large external capacitors (coupling and bypass) have negligible reactance and act as short circuits.</li><li><strong>Parasitic Capacitances:</strong> At these same frequencies, the signal frequency is low enough that the very small internal device capacitances (parasitic capacitances) have extremely high reactance and act as open circuits.</li><li><strong>Capacitive Reactance:</strong> The behavior is governed by \(X_C = \frac{1}{2\pi f C}\), demonstrating the inverse relationship between the capacitance value and its reactance at a given frequency.</li></ul></div>

Given a 5-bit shift register counter initialized with logic values: \[\begin{aligned}
(Q_{1}, Q_{2}, Q_{3}, Q_{4}, Q_{5}) &amp;= (0, 1, 0, 1, 0) &amp;
\end{aligned}\] The circuit forms a Ring Counter feedback shift register where \(D_{1} = \overline{Q}_{5}\), and \(D_{n} = Q_{n-1}\) for \(n \geq 2\). Tracing the state sequence over successive clock periods: 1.3 <div class="table-scroll"><table class="qtable"><tr><td><strong>CLK</strong></td><td>\(Q_{1}\)</td><td>\(Q_{2}\)</td><td>\(Q_{3}\)</td><td>\(Q_{4}\)</td><td>\(Q_{5}\)</td><td><strong>Output</strong> \(Y = Q_{3} + Q_{5}\)</td></tr><tr><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td><strong>1</strong></td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td><strong>1</strong></td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td><strong>1</strong></td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr></table></div> The sequence repeats its periodic behavior every \(5T\) intervals. \[\begin{aligned}
\text{Total Period } (T_{\text{total}}) &amp;= 5T &amp;\\
\text{ON time during one full period } (T_{\text{ON}}) &amp;= 3T &amp;
\end{aligned}\] The average power dissipation \(P_{\text{avg}}\) in the resistor \(R = 10\,\text{k}\Omega\) with a supply voltage \(V = 5\,\text{V}\) is calculated as: \[P_{\text{avg}} = \frac{V^{2}}{R} \times \frac{T_{\text{ON}}}{T_{\text{total}}} = \frac{5^{2}}{10\,\text{k}\Omega} \times \frac{3T}{5T} = \frac{25}{10} \times 0.6 = 1.5\,\text{ms}\] \[\boxed{1.5}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>Average power dissipation formula for periodic pulse waveforms: \(P_{\text{avg}} = \frac{V^{2}}{R} \times \frac{T_{\text{ON}}}{T}\).</li><li>Ring and Johnson counters shift data linearly on each active clock edge.</li></ul></div>

To understand the execution of the RLC instruction, let us break down how the 8085 microprocessor processes data rotation: <ul><li><strong>Initial Conditions:</strong><br/><ul><li>Accumulator contents: \(A = \text{A7H} = (1010\,0111)_2\)</li><li>Carry Flag: \(\text{CY} = 0\)</li></ul></li><li><strong>Operation of RLC (Rotate Left Accumulator):</strong><br/>Unlike instructions that rotate data through the carry flag (such as RAL), RLC rotates the 8 bits of the accumulator circularly to the left. The most significant bit (\(D_7\)) has a <strong>dual destination:</strong> <ol><li>It is shifted directly into the least significant bit position (\(D_0\)).</li><li>It overwrites the current value of the Carry Flag (\(\text{CY}\)).</li></ol></li></ul> <img src="img/738610d9ff094e37.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,256px)"> \[\begin{array}{rcccccccc}
    \text{Bit Position:} &amp; D_7 &amp; D_6 &amp; D_5 &amp; D_4 &amp; D_3 &amp; D_2 &amp; D_1 &amp; D_0 \\
    \text{Final Value:} &amp; 0 &amp; 1 &amp; 0 &amp; 0 &amp; 1 &amp; 1 &amp; 1 &amp; \mathbf{1}
    \end{array}\] CY: 1 Grouping the final binary string into 4-bit nibbles to determine the hexadecimal value: <ul><li>Higher nibble: \((0100)_2 = 4_\text{H}\)</li><li>Lower nibble: \((1111)_2 = F_\text{H}\)</li></ul> Therefore, the final accumulator content is \((4F)_H\) and the carry flag \(\text{CY}\) is \(1\). \[\boxed{\text{(D) $4F$ and 1}}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li><strong>RLC vs RAL:</strong> RLC moves \(D_7\) directly to \(D_0\) and \(\text{CY}\). RAL moves \(D_7\) to \(\text{CY}\), and the old value of \(\text{CY}\) goes to \(D_0\).</li><li>The initial state of the carry flag (\(\text{CY} = 0\)) has no influence on the final result of an RLC command.</li></ul></div>

